Stage 1: Wound-Induced Propagation (Channel 1)
Component: Root Cuttings (Apple)

Mechanism: Physical wounding stimulates pericycle cell division, triggering de novo shoot organogenesis.

Role in Loop: Acts as the initial propagation trigger, leveraging natural injury responses to rapidly generate foundational vegetative tissue from temperate woody perennials.

Stage 2: Genetic Refinement & Haploid Induction (Channel 2)
Component: Anther Cultures (Citrus)

Mechanism: Microspore or anther manipulation induces haploid organogenesis, bypassing normal gametophytic dead-ends to accelerate homozygous line development.

Role in Loop: Provides a genetic purification and stabilization checkpoint, ensuring that the propagated material possesses fixed traits and hybrid vigor before scaling.

Stage 3: Subterranean Storage & Sink Development (Channel 6)
Component: Sweet Potato Roots (Root Vegetables)

Mechanism: Adventitious roots undergo localized secondary thickening, radial expansion, and intensive starch accumulation.

Role in Loop: Shifts the biological focus from vegetative shoot growth to dense biomass and caloric storage, maximizing carbohydrate yield per square meter.

Stage 4: Closed-Loop Environmental Integration (Channel 7)
Component: Wheat Cultivation Systems (Space / BLSS)

Mechanism: Complete life-cycle crop production (germination, vegetative growth, anthesis, and grain formation) within a controlled, recycled life support environment.

Role in Loop: Represents the culmination of the pipeline—integrating propagation, genetic stability, and high-yield storage into a self-sustaining agricultural system capable of operating independently in microgravity or isolated planetary habitats.
